<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zirid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zirid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zirid dynasty was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> Berber dynasty that governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, roughly present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> after the Fatimids moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Between about 1048 and 1052 the Zirid governor abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy. This defection severed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s North African domain and was an early stage in Fatimid decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zirid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zirid dynasty" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R Prazeres (using file uploaded by Kabyle20 as template) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zirid_control_circa_980_(version_2).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Zirid</b> defection of around 1048, when the Ifriqiyan governor al-Mu'izz ibn Badis abandoned Ismaili allegiance for Sunni orthodoxy, permanently cut off the caliphate's North African hinterland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1117 the emir Rafi ibn Makkan quarrelled with the <b>Zirids of Mahdia</b> over his merchant ship and sought help from Roger II of Sicily, an early step in Norman intervention in Africa.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-jami-emirate-of-gabes">Banu Jami emirate of Gabes</a></li><li><blockquote>The Khurasanids ruled Tunis and its hinterland as a small Sunni city-state, at first recognising the Hammadid emirate as overlord to secure protection from the Arab tribes and the <b>Zirids of Mahdia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-khurasan">Banu Khurasan</a></li><li><blockquote>Hammad ibn Buluggin, a son of the <b>Zirid</b> founder, governed the western lands of the Zirid emirate and founded the fortified city of Qal'at Beni Hammad in the Hodna mountains around 1007 to 1008.</blockquote><a href="/state/hammadid-emirate">Hammadid emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>His admiral George of Antioch took Tripoli in 1146, and in 1148 Mahdia, Sousse and Sfax fell as the <b>Zirid</b> ruler al-Hasan fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-africa">Kingdom of Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Zirid</b> rule Kairouan reached its height as a centre of trade, crafts and Maliki scholarship, and Ifriqiya exported grain, olive oil and textiles.</blockquote><a href="/state/zirid-emirate">Zirid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
